已经不再适用了。但抛开我的处境不谈，我认为这是一项巨大的资源。我认为它发挥最佳作用的地方是，我们刚才讨论了“向与你有点不同的人寻求建议”的想法，当你感到困境时。我认为ChatGPT基本上可以作为世界上数百万不同智能体汇聚的智慧发挥作用，因为它在梳理互联网上可用的内容。所以对我来说，生成式人工智能是一种伟大的打破困境的力量，可以用作任何过程的第一步，无论这个过程可能是什么。所以我认为这通常是我们陷入困境的地方。它是从零到一的过程。

38
00:23:23.000 --> 00:24:23.000
And an example of this is if I'm sitting and struggling to write the beginning sentence of a chapter or the beginning paragraph of a chapter and I'm writing a book, which is something I've done a few times now, and I've often been stuck at the beginning of a chapter, it's a difficult thing to do. One thing you can do, and I have done this with ChatGPT occasionally, is you can say to it, I'm writing a book chapter, this is what the chapter is about, you can give it a brief description. Tell me five different ways that you could write this chapter, you could begin the chapter. That doesn't mean you're plagiarizing, but what it's doing is it's getting you to think about some different options, the way you might, if you'd spoken to five of your best writer friends, and they said, well, here's what I would do. instantly, without having to check schedules and so on, you have this device or this platform that's telling you here are five different ways to do it. And the nice thing about chat GPT is if you say to it, you know, let's imagine that the last thing you said was wrong or let's improve on what you just did. It'll do that for you. And so you can push it and prod it in different directions.
一个例子是，如果我坐着，艰难地写一章的开头句子或开头段落，而我正在写一本书，这是我已经做过好几次的事情，而且我经常在章节的开始感到卡壳，这是一件困难的事情。你可以做的一件事（我偶尔也会用ChatGPT这样做）是你可以对它说：“我正在写一本书的一个章节，这是这一章的内容，”你可以给它一个简短的描述。“告诉我写这一章、可以开始这一章的五种不同方法。”这并不意味着你在抄袭，但它做的是让你思考一些不同的选项，就像如果你和你的五个最好的作家朋友交谈，他们说：“嗯，这就是我会做的。”瞬间，不需要核对日程安排等等，你就有这个设备或这个平台告诉你这里有五种不同的方法。而ChatGPT的好处是，如果你对它说：“你知道，让我们假设你刚才说的话是错的，或者让我们改进你刚才做的事情，”它会为你这样做。所以你可以从不同的方向推动和敦促它。

39
00:24:23.000 --> 00:25:03.000
So I find it a tremendous brainstorming partner for that early phase where you feel stuck and where you're trying to move from, you know, zero miles an hour to maybe 10 miles an hour. From 10 to 60, if that's what your goal is, you might have to do that on your own, but it gets you started. Let's talk for a moment about the role of fear in being stuck. Do people sometimes get stuck just because they're afraid of change or of the unknown? Yeah, so this survey that I've been running on people around the world, one of the questions I ask is tell me what it feels like as you think about being stuck. and it's not surprising, I think, that the emotions they feel are negative.
所以我发现它是一个极好的头脑风暴伙伴，适用于你感到困境的早期阶段，以及你试图从每小时0英里移动到也许每小时10英里的阶段。从10到60，如果那是你的目标，你可能得靠自己完成，但它让你开始了。让我们花一点时间谈谈恐惧在陷入困境中的作用。人们有时会仅仅因为害怕改变或未知而陷入困境吗？是的，我在世界各地的人们中进行的这项调查中，我问的一个问题是：告诉当你想起被困住时的感觉是怎样的。

40
00:25:03.000 --> 00:25:27.000
And fear comes up quite often. Anxiety comes up quite often. There's a real sense of isolation and loneliness, despite the fact that it seems near universal. It's often hidden from us that others are stuck as well. And I find one of the best ways to get over that fear of feeling stuck is to shine sunlight on this idea that being stuck is universal. And in fact, the beginning of the book, I spent quite a long time trying to
毫不奇怪，他们感受到的情绪是负面的。恐惧经常出现。焦虑经常出现。有一种真正的孤立感和孤独感，尽管这种感觉似乎几乎是普遍的。我们常常被隐藏了这样一个事实：其他人也被困住了。我发现克服对被困住的恐惧的最好方法之一，就是将阳光照耀在“被困住是普遍现象”这个观点上。事实上，在书的开头，我花了相当长的时间试图

41
00:25:27.000 --> 00:26:09.000
figure out whether there were some classic examples of tremendously successful people from the outside who once you look at the process that was hidden from the world, you find, turns out they were very, very afraid, very stuck, very uncomfortable until they weren't. And you find this with some of the biggest businesses in the world. I talk about Amazon and Airbnb. You find it with some of the most successful artists. I specifically focus at the beginning of the book on Brie Larson, the actress who won an Academy Award for Best Actress. And, you know, I, I, I, I'm yet to find someone who says, in all honesty, I was never stuck. It was always easy. It was plain sailing. It was not an issue for me at all. And so, yes, going back to your question,
弄清楚是否有这样一些经典例子：表面上极其成功的人，一旦你看一下对世界隐藏的过程，你就会发现，原来他们也非常非常害怕，非常被困住，非常不舒服，直到他们不再如此。你在一些世界上最大的企业中也会发现这一点。我谈到了亚马逊和爱彼迎（Airbnb）。你在一些最成功的艺术家身上也能发现这一点。我特别在书的开头把重点放在了布丽·拉尔森（Brie Larson）身上，这位获得奥斯卡最佳女主角的女演员。而且，你知道，我，我，我，我还没有遇到过一个人坦诚地说：“我从来没有被困住过。一切总是那么容易。那是风平浪静的。对我来说根本不是问题。”所以，是的，回到你的问题。

42
00:26:09.000 --> 00:26:54.000
I think fear is a very big factor that prevents people from moving forward. But so much of that comes from the sense that this is a sort of unique experience that I'm going through. And once you get past that, it usually feels, in my experience, quite liberating. And people do move forward. when a person's been stuck on something for a long time, how do they know when it's time to quit versus whether they should continue to try to push through? Is there some kind of a cost-benefit analysis that people could do in such cases? Yeah, it's a very interesting and important question. It's one of the big questions that comes up. In the book, I think I push quite firmly on the idea of persevering in general because I think a lot of the research suggests there's a lot of value
我认为恐惧是阻碍人们前进的一个非常大的因素。但其中很大一部分原因来自于一种感觉，即我正在经历的是一种独特的体验。一旦你跨过了这个坎，以我的经验来看，通常会觉得相当解放。人们也确实在向前迈进。当一个人被某件事卡住很久时，他们怎么知道什么时候该放弃，什么时候该继续坚持下去？在这种情况人们可以做某种成本效益分析吗？是的，这是一个非常有趣且重要的问题。这是经常出现的大问题之一。在这本书中，我认为我相当坚定地主张总的来说要坚持不懈，因为我认为很多研究表明，坚持下去有很大的价值

43
00:26:54.000 --> 00:27:18.000
to persevering beyond the point where things get difficult. There are also some phenomenal books, including a book by Annie Duke called Quit, which is about the opposite. How do you know when to stop? And obviously, a wise assessment suggests that you can't always push forward and you can't always quit. You need to know how to distinguish the two. One of the useful pieces of advice that I've come across from people who seem to find
即坚持到事情变得困难的那个点之后。也有一些非常棒的书籍，包括安妮·杜克（Annie Duke）写的《放弃》（Quit），这本书讲的是相反的内容。你怎么知道什么时候该停下来？很显然，明智的评估表明，你不能总是硬着头皮前进，也不能总是说放弃就放弃。你需要知道如何区分这两者。我从那些似乎能很好地找到中间平衡点的人那里听到过一个很有用的建议

44
00:27:18.000 --> 00:27:56.000
the middle ground quite well is they will describe a sense of convergence on a an endpoint that they desire. So if you're learning a language or a skill or you're trying to produce a creative idea or you have a business or you're trying out a career or you're trying a new major in college, whatever it might be, you'll probably run into some hardship. You can't quit at the first sign of hardship. That's what a lot of the research suggests. But if all you're doing is slogging through the process and you don't feel like you're converging on something promising, rewarding, that makes you feel good, that brings you the emotional returns you're looking for, then at some point you do need to quit.
他们会描述一种向他们渴望的终点收敛的感觉。因此，如果你在学习一门语言或一项技能，或者你正试图产生一个创意的想法，或者你拥有一家企业，或者你正在尝试一项职业，或者你在大学里尝试一个新的专业，无论它是什么，你都可能会遇到一些困难。你不能在遇到困难的第一迹象时就放弃。这就是许多研究所表明的。但如果你所做的只是艰难地熬过这个过程，而且你并不觉得自己在向某个充满希望、有回报、让你感觉良好、能带来你所寻找的情感回报的事情靠拢，那么在某个时刻，你确实需要放弃。

45
00:27:56.000 --> 00:28:25.000
And so one of the things you can do, sometimes there's an objective standard that helps you do this, but one thing you can do is to ask, am I getting closer to the goal over time, am I converging? Is the gap between my current skill level or my experience now and where I'd like to be shrinking or is it staying the same size or is it even getting larger? And once it starts getting larger and you feel like you're getting further and further from where you want to be, I think you start to make a good, case for quitting. But I think the idea of quitting as an empty idea doesn't make much sense.
所以你能做的事情之一——有时有一个客观标准可以帮助你做到这一点——但你能做的一件事就是问：随着时间的推移，我离目标更近了吗？我是在收敛吗？我当前的技能水平或现在的体验与我想达到的目标之间的差距是在缩小，还是保持不变，甚至在变大？一旦这个差距开始变大，你觉得离你想去的地方越来越远，我认为这就为放弃提供了充分的理由。不过，我认为把放弃看作是一个空洞的概念是没有什么意义的。

46
00:28:25.000 --> 00:28:58.000
For me, it's always, what are you quitting to? What happens next? And so in my experience, when I've moved through different processes and when I've decided not to do something, the best thing you can do is really to line up the next thing and wherever possible, especially if it's a professional or creative endeavor. Now, in addition to your academic work, you do a lot of consulting with companies. and organizations. I'm wondering if the advice you give them is similar to the advice that you give to individuals in terms of how to get unstuck.
对我来说，这总是：你放弃是为了转向什么？接下来会发生什么？因此，根据我的经验，当我经历不同的过程并决定不做某事时，你能做的最好的事情确实是把下一件事情安排好，无论什么时候都要尽可能做到，特别是当它是一项职业或创意事业时。现在，除了学术工作之外，你还为公司和组织提供大量的咨询。我想知道，你给他们的建议是否类似于你给个人的建议，也就是关于如何摆脱困境。

47
00:28:58.000 --> 00:29:31.000
It's broadly similar. In the book, I focus on individuals to a large extent, but I also talk about organizations and management and marketing problems specifically. A lot of the ideas, I think, are leadership-oriented. How do you put together a team? How do you put together a brain trust so that you maximize the chance of an organization unsticking. So there are some ideas that I think are germane to business decision making, maybe more than everyday life. But I always find, you know, I think one of the big skills that
大体上是相似的。在书中，我在很大程度上关注个人，但我也专门讨论了组织、管理和营销方面的问题。我认为许多想法都是以领导力为导向的。你如何组建一个团队？你如何组建一个智囊团，以最大化地提高组织摆脱困境的几率？因此，有些想法我认为与商业决策息息相关，可能比日常生活更是如此。但我总是发现，你知道，我认为心理学家随着时间推移学到的重大技能之一就是

48
00:29:31.000 --> 00:30:11.000
we psychologists learn over time is that useful ideas have a lot of applications. And the best thing you can do is try to figure out where they apply. And often they apply more broadly than your instincts might suggest. And so I think a lot of the ideas, even, so here's an example, if I'm putting together a team and I'm trying to work out what sort of employees to put on that team, another question that you might ask, the individual version of that might be, I'm trying to put together a group of trusted people that I can turn to when I feel like I don't know how to move forward. And it turns out the same rules about composing that team at the workplace and in life, in general, hold. And so I think you can learn a tremendous amount from the business ideas and also
有用的想法有很多应用。你能做的最好的事情就是试着找出它们在哪里适用。通常它们的适用范围比你的直觉所暗示的要广泛得多。因此，我认为许多想法——举个例子，如果我要组建一个团队，并且我正在努力弄清楚应该把什么样的员工放进这个团队里，你可能会问的另一个问题，其个人版本可能是：我正在努力组建一群值得信赖的人，当我觉得自己不知道该如何前进时，我可以向他们求助。事实证明，关于在职场中和生活中总体上组建该团队的规则是相同的。因此，我认为你可以从商业理念中学到极多，并且

49
00:30:11.000 --> 00:30:40.000
the business ideas, I think, gain a lot from just what it is to be an, individual human being navigating the world. Is there any other advice that we haven't discussed that you'd want to leave with our listeners today? I think one thing I'll say is we, in childhood, I have a five-year-old and a seven-year-old, and in childhood there's a lot of curiosity. We find the world a curious place. We ask a huge number of questions.
我也认为商业理念从作为一个在世界上航行的普通个人中获益良多。我们今天还有什么没有讨论过的建议想留给我们的听众吗？我想说的一点是，我们在童年时期——我有一个五岁和一个七岁的孩子——童年时期有很强的好奇心。我们发现世界是一个充满好奇的地方。我们提出了大量的问题。

50
00:30:40.000 --> 00:31:13.000
We're very rarely satisfied by the first, second or third answers we get. And so we push forward constantly and, you know, kids are never satisfied with the answers they get. At some point between being a child and being an adult that shifts, we start to assume that common wisdom is common wisdom for a reason. Adults don't question that much. You know, you see other people doing something. You assume it's the right way where a child might say, why is that? And it turns out there are some adults who are natural, I call them experimentalists. It's a sort of philosophy of questioning everything.
我们很少对得到的第一个、第二个或第三个答案感到满意。因此，我们不断地向前推进，你知道，孩子们对他们得到的答案永远不会满足。在从孩子成长为成年人的某个时间点，情况发生了转变，我们开始认为常识之所以成为常识是有原因的。成年人不会提出那么多质疑。你知道，你看到别人在做什么。你就会认为那是正确的方法，而一个孩子可能会问：“为什么会这样？”事实证明，有些成年人天生就是——我称他们为实验主义者。这是一种质疑一切的哲学。

51
00:31:13.000 --> 00:31:39.000
and wondering whether with the right experiment there might be a better way. Is there a better way for me to communicate with my loved ones? Is there a better way for me to work in the workplace? Is there a better way for me even narrowly to learn how to play a particular piano piece or paint a particular painting? It doesn't really matter the task. But one of the chapters I talk about these people who are chronic experimentalists and how, for some of them, it unlocks tremendous potential.
并且想知道通过正确的实验是否会有更好的方法。我有没有更好的方法与我爱的人沟通？我在职场中工作有没有更好的方法？甚至在狭义上，我有没有更好的方法来学习弹奏一首特定的钢琴曲或画一幅特定的画？任务是什么并不重要。但在其中一章中，我谈到了这些被称为“慢性实验主义者”的人，以及对他们中的一些人来说，这如何释放出巨大的潜力。

52
00:31:39.000 --> 00:32:06.000
I talk about an Olympic swimmer who learned to be an Olympic swimmer at that level of proficiency by essentially saying, hey, the way everyone else is swimming the backstroke is not the best way. He pioneered a completely new technique. And there are other people in other domains too. So I think that's a really valuable approach to the world that keeps us, A, young, and B, makes us open, or keeps us open to the potential to do better in general. And I think it's one of the best ways to prevent yourself from getting stuck in the first place
我讲到了一位奥运会游泳运动员，他通过基本上说“嘿，其他人游仰泳的方法并不是最好的方法”，从而以那种熟练程度学会成为了一名奥运会游泳运动员。他开创了一种全新的技术。其他领域也有这样的人。所以我认为这是一种对世界非常有价值的方法，它能让我们，A，保持年轻，B，让我们保持开放的心态，或者保持对普遍做得更好的潜力的开放态度。我认为这是防止自己一开始就陷入困境的最好方法之一

53
00:32:06.000 --> 00:32:30.000
is to always say, oh yeah, this is starting to feel a little bit sticky. it's not working the way I'd like. I think I need to consider alternatives. And that's, I think, a very useful piece of advice. And what's next for you? What else are you working on besides stuckness? Yeah, so I'm constantly working on a whole lot of ideas. In fact, I talk about this in the book, that I have this document that's called
那就是随时对自己说：“哦，是的，这开始觉得有点卡壳了。它的运作方式不如我所愿。我想我需要考虑替代方案。”我认为这是一个非常实用的建议。接下来你有什么打算？除了摆脱困境，你还在研究什么？是的，我一直在研究一整套的想法。事实上，我在书里也谈到了这一点，我有一份文件叫做

54
00:32:30.000 --> 00:33:01.000
research ideas and another document called book ideas. And what they essentially are is 20 years worth of interesting ideas that have either come to me that I've come across in the world, it would take me 100 lifetimes to actually pursue all of them. So at the moment, I'm very invested in the book and in the question of how we can get unstuck in lots of different contexts. But at some point, I'm sure I'll return to those documents and figure out what's next. Well, I want to thank you for joining me today, Dr. Alter. This has been really interesting.
研究想法，还有另一份文件叫做书的想法。它们本质上是长达20年的有趣想法，这些想法要么是我自己想到的，要么是我在世界上遇到的，我需要100辈子的时间才能真正把它们全部付诸实践。所以目前，我非常投入到这本书中，投入到我们如何在许多不同的情境中摆脱困境的问题中。但在某个时刻，我确信我会回到那些文件中去弄清楚接下来要做什么。嗯，非常感谢你今天能来，奥尔特博士。这真的非常有趣。

